<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Négritude</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1930s</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Negritude</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Négritude is a framework of critique and literary theory developed in the 1930s, mainly by French-speaking Black intellectuals and writers, to raise Black consciousness across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and its diaspora. Its founders included the Martinican poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aime-cesaire">Aimé Césaire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-sedar-senghor">Léopold Sédar Senghor</a>, later the first president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>, and Léon Damas of French Guiana. Césaire first used the word in its present sense in 1935 in L'Étudiant noir, a magazine he had started in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> with fellow students. Rejecting colonialism, racism and Eurocentrism, its writers drew on Marxism and Surrealism and celebrated African history and culture.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Négritude takes its name from the French nègre, a word that had been used mainly as an insult, with the suffix -itude. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aime-cesaire">Aimé Césaire</a> deliberately took the word as a positive term and first used it in its present sense in the third issue, of May–June 1935, of L'Étudiant noir, a magazine he started in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-sedar-senghor">Léopold Sédar Senghor</a>, Léon Damas and other students. His first published work, Conscience Raciale et Révolution Sociale, rejected assimilation, since to assimilate was to accept a culture that held African culture to be barbaric.</p><p>Earlier influences included the Haitian anthropologist Anténor Firmin, who in 1885 rebutted Arthur de Gobineau's theory of racial inequality, and the Harlem Renaissance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langston-hughes">Langston Hughes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claude-mckay">Claude McKay</a> and others. Paulette and Jane Nardal introduced these writers to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> students at their Clamart Salon, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paulette-nardal">Paulette Nardal</a> helped begin La Revue du Monde Noir in 1931. Paulette later complained that she and her sister were not acknowledged. Césaire called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> the place where négritude first stood up.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Its founders were dissatisfied with the Afro-French experience in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and shared a revolt against racism and colonial injustice. The writers opposed colonialism and Eurocentrism, asserted that Black people had a history and culture equal to others, and took pride in being Black. They drew on Marxist ideas and Surrealist style and showed Heideggerian influence. Each had his own aims. Césaire stressed accepting blackness as the way to decolonise the mind and blamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a> for the inferiority complex of Black people. Senghor wanted Black people in French lands to have a seat at the French table as equals, and later used négritude to seek a universal valuation of African people. Damas defended black qualities and addressed the colonised in general.</p><p>Neither Césaire nor Senghor at first envisaged independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and Césaire called for the departmentalisation of Martinique. Two anthologies were central: Damas's Poètes d'expression française 1900–1945 of 1946 and Senghor's Anthologie de la nouvelle poésie nègre et malgache de langue française of 1948. A visual movement followed, and the Dakar School in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>, active from 1960 to 1974, was directly influenced by the philosophy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-paul-sartre">Jean-Paul Sartre</a>'s essay Orphée Noir, the preface to Senghor's anthology, called négritude an anti-racist racism and carried it to French intellectual debate. Some Black writers of the 1960s thought it not militant enough, and Keorapetse Kgositsile held that it was too tied to a European view of blackness. Chinua Achebe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wole-soyinka">Wole Soyinka</a> opposed it, Soyinka writing that a tiger does not proclaim its tigerness but jumps on its prey. Négritude inspired Afro-Surrealism, Créolité and Antillanité, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frantz-fanon">Frantz Fanon</a> often referred to it. Scholars including Souleymane Bachir Diagne have since revived the study of it as a philosophy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Négritude” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Paris, students from the French Caribbean and Africa, among them Aimé Césaire and Léopold Sédar Senghor, developed <b>Négritude</b>, an affirmation of the worth of Black cultures against colonial claims to a civilising mission.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · Aims</a></li></ul></section>
